WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.200
(slow bright music)

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:04.196
- [Narrator] A century ago
100,000 tigers roamed the earth.

00:00:04.196 --> 00:00:06.180
Today those numbers have declined

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:08.480
to just about 4,000.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.210
But in the forest of Eastern Thailand,

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:12.730
conservationists have
found new signs of hope

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:14.583
for this iconic species.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:17.630
This Great Big Story was made possible

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:21.892
by B. Grimm, empowering
the world compassionately.

00:00:21.892 --> 00:00:25.890
(birds twittering)

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:28.640
Until recently it was believed
that the Eastern forests

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:30.700
of Thailand didn't have enough wild tigers

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.573
to maintain a breeding population.

00:00:33.502 --> 00:00:35.416
But a group of conservationists led

00:00:35.416 --> 00:00:38.810
by Panthera, the Thai
department of National parks

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:40.860
and the Freeland Foundation went out

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.183
into the jungle to find
out for themselves.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:47.754
- We set up around 83 stations of cameras.

00:00:47.754 --> 00:00:49.435
Each station has two cameras.

00:00:49.435 --> 00:00:53.140
The general goal of setting
up the camera trap grid was

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:55.240
to estimate how many tigers are there.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:57.440
What potential is there
for them to recover

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:58.426
in that area.

00:00:58.426 --> 00:01:00.690
Camera trapping is always fun

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:02.460
'cause you have these cameras out for 45,

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:04.222
60 days in the field.

00:01:04.222 --> 00:01:06.440
And they're out there
every day taking pictures,

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:08.850
so you bring them back and you've got

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:10.520
you know lots of people looking

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:11.450
at all the photos.

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:13.020
Clicking through what's next,

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:14.080
what's next, what's next.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:18.940
And we discovered that we
have a breeding population

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:19.850
of tigers there.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:22.630
This is kind of the first
evidence of breeding

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:24.654
for at least I think a decade.

00:01:24.654 --> 00:01:28.673
It's a really amazing thing
to have been able to see

00:01:28.673 --> 00:01:30.033
and to document.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:36.222
Tigers are a symbol of a
healthy intact ecosystem.

00:01:36.222 --> 00:01:39.870
To have tigers disappear from the wild,

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:41.210
especially when we have the means

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:43.220
to stop that from happening, would be

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:44.253
an absolute tragedy.

00:01:45.087 --> 00:01:47.662
So although the survey
results are great news

00:01:47.662 --> 00:01:50.490
we really do need to keep the pressure on.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:53.089
I mean there's the threats
aren't necessarily abating

00:01:53.089 --> 00:01:54.340
at those areas.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:55.870
So the good work that's continuing

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:57.713
with patrolling on the ground,

00:01:57.713 --> 00:02:00.130
law enforcement, working on trying

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:02.774
to give tigers a chance to breed

00:02:02.774 --> 00:02:05.220
to have the area they need to persist

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:06.599
is really important.

00:02:06.599 --> 00:02:09.599
(slow bright music)

